# Point clangd at a compile database that no build script ever touches.
#
# Generate or refresh it with:
#   ./native/gen_clangd_db.sh          (see "Editor setup (clangd)" in docs/building.md)
#
# Why not native/build: that tree is the shim build's, and native/build.sh `rm -rf`s it on every
# run. Worse, the blessed build path (native/local_build_wrapper.sh) runs in a container, so the
# database it leaves behind holds container-absolute paths (/workspace/...) and is root-owned --
# clangd resolves nothing and repairing it needs sudo. native/build-clangd is written only by
# gen_clangd_db.sh, so it survives every build.
#
# Why that script instead of a single cmake configure: native/CMakeLists.txt builds the JNI shim
# only when ET_BUILD_QA and ET_BUILD_BENCH are both OFF (the Catch2 units and harnesses are
# JVM-free and deliberately need no JDK), so no one configure covers both jni/ and test/. The
# script runs both and merges the two databases.
#
# STALENESS: the database is refreshed only when you run that script. After a bump of
# native/cmake/EtRuntimePin.cmake it keeps resolving against the OLD runtime headers, silently and
# with no warning. Re-run the script as part of the pin-bump procedure (noted in CLAUDE.md).
#
# Only this file is committed. native/build-clangd/ is git-ignored: its entries carry absolute
# paths (the build tree, the fetched runtime's include dir, the JDK, and the .pte fixture paths
# passed as -D macro values), so it is valid only on the machine that generated it.
CompileFlags:
  CompilationDatabase: native/build-clangd
